# Valgrind memcheck suppressions for the Umbra (Shadow) game engine.
#
# Scope: third-party library allocation noise that valgrind's pointer
# heuristics classify as "possibly lost" but that is not a real defect in
# engine or test code. Used by the `linux-valgrind` CI job (see
# .github/workflows/ci.yml), which on a TRIAL basis treats "possibly lost"
# as an error so no real leak class can slip past both valgrind and LSan.
#
# Policy baked into every entry below:
#   * match-leak-kinds: possible
# Definite and indirect leaks are NEVER suppressed here - a real leak in
# engine, test, or library code still fails the job. These blocks only
# narrow the "possibly lost" class to the known SDL2/box2d allocation
# paths, so genuine possible-loss bugs in first-party code still red.
#
# These were written from the well-known SDL2 / box2d allocation sites
# (box2d routes all of its allocations through b2Alloc -> malloc; SDL2's
# thread-local error buffer is allocated lazily by SDL_GetErrBuf and is
# intentionally never freed). After the first real Linux CI run, refine
# the frames to match the exact stacks valgrind reports if new library
# noise appears:
#
#   make memcheck-suppressions    # Linux; runs --gen-suppressions=all and
#                                 # appends new possible-only blocks here
#
# Manual equivalent (used only when the automated harvest is not enough):
#
#   valgrind --leak-check=full --show-leak-kinds=all --gen-suppressions=all \
#       ./build-valgrind/sdl_app_tests
#
# then trim the generated blocks to the library frames only (never to
# engine/test frames) and keep match-leak-kinds: possible. The automated
# helper applies exactly that policy (possible-only, deduped, validated).

# SDL2 thread-local error buffer - allocated on first SDL_SetError and
# intentionally never freed (thread-exit cleanup only). Classified
# "possibly lost" by valgrind; not a real leak.
{
   sdl2_threadlocal_error_buffer
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: possible
   fun:malloc
   fun:SDL_GetErrBuf
   ...
}

# box2d internal allocation via b2Alloc - the vendored box2d allocator
# (b2BlockAllocator / b2ArenaAllocator chunk arenas and per-world pools).
# Freed with the owning b2World; valgrind's interior-pointer heuristics
# can classify the chunk arenas "possibly lost" at process exit.
# Suppressed only for the "possible" kind.
{
   box2d_internal_b2Alloc_arena_chunks
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: possible
   fun:malloc
   fun:b2Alloc
   ...
}
